Lumia 1520 (RM-940) -- WCD9320 enumeration negative control + revision read
Kernel 6.16.12 #116-postmarketos-qcom-msm8974, boot-1520-taiko-enum.img (r115)
2026-07-30

Both tests run on ONE fresh boot. A fresh boot is mandatory: after a
successful enumeration the SLIMbus core caches the logical address with
is_laddr_valid = true, and only slim_report_absent() clears it -- an ordinary
module unload does not. Reload alone would let slim_get_logical_addr()
short-circuit, so a codec held in reset could appear to enumerate.

Pre-test state: transport up automatically at t=4.53s, ADSP running,
217:a0:1:0 present from DT with driver NONE, zero WCD messages.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
TEST A -- hold_reset=1 : the codec MUST NOT enumerate
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

[ 139.088272] WCD9320: probe (hold_reset=1)
[ 139.088696] WCD9320: expected (from DT) EA = 00 01 a0 00 17 02
[ 139.096469] WCD9320: 7 supplies enabled, reset asserted
[ 139.096570] WCD9320: NEGATIVE CONTROL -- reset deliberately held asserted; enumeration must FAIL
[ 139.097343] wcd9320-enum 217:a0:1:0: Failed to get logical address
[ 139.097473] WCD9320: cleanup -- reset asserted, 7 supplies disabled

  device_up occurrences         : 0     (required 0)
  Failed to get logical address : 1
  assigned logical address      : 0     (required 0)
  chip id reads                 : 0     (required 0)
  driver bound                  : NONE
  cleanup unwind ran            : 1
  ADSP after                    : running

The devm_add_action_or_reset() unwind fired correctly. That matters: the
logical-address query runs inside slim_device_probe() AFTER the driver's
probe() returned success, so the bus probe returns -EPROBE_DEFER and the
driver never fully binds -- remove() would not have run.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
TEST B -- hold_reset=0 : the codec MUST enumerate
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

[ 189.191405] WCD9320: probe (hold_reset=0)
[ 189.196753] WCD9320: 7 supplies enabled, reset asserted
[ 189.249110] WCD9320: reset released (low 20 ms, settle 20 ms)
[ 189.250065] WCD9320: *** device_up *** codec is present on the bus
[ 189.250452] WCD9320: reported (from silicon) EA = 00 01 a0 00 17 02
[ 189.251105] WCD9320: assigned logical address = 0xcb

  device_up occurrences    : 1
  assigned logical address : 1
  driver bound             : wcd9320-enum
  ADSP after               : running

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
RESULT: enumeration is proven
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Same boot, same code path, same supplies; only the reset line differs.
Reset asserted -> no logical address, no device_up. Reset released ->
EA answered, logical address assigned, device_up. This closes the gap that
source analysis alone left open.

Note the assigned logical address was 0xcb here and 0x00 in the previous
session. A value that varies between boots is further evidence it is a real
assignment from the manager rather than a constant or a null read.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
REVISION READ: FAILED -- cause identified, not yet fixed
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

[ 189.251400] WCD9320: chip id bytes 0x04..0x08 = 00 00 00 00, version = 0x00
[ 189.251492] WCD9320: id_major = 0x0000, id_minor = 0x0000
[ 189.251570] WCD9320: major 0x0000 is not Taiko (expected 0x0102)

slim_read() returned success, so the transaction completed and the data is
genuinely zero. The cause is an addressing error in this probe, not a codec
or bus problem:

  downstream wcd9xxx_slim_read_device():
      msg.start_offset = WCD9XXX_REGISTER_START_OFFSET + reg;
  and
      #define WCD9XXX_REGISTER_START_OFFSET 0x800

The WCD9xxx register map is offset by 0x800 within the value-element space.
This probe read raw value elements 0x04..0x08, which are 2 KB below the
register map, so they read as zero. The chip id is at 0x804..0x807 and the
version at 0x808.

The part is therefore still NOT identified from its registers. Its identity
rests on the enumeration address (manf 0x217, prod 0x0a0), which matches the
downstream Taiko value exactly, and on the board wiring. Do not record the
zeros as a revision.

Unrelated and harmless, seen once: "failed while handling packet from
1:16388" after the module unload.
